[Music]  having been on YouTube for more than  half of a decade now I've gotten a lot  of comments a lot of questions in the  comments section and some of them fall  into certain classes many of them are  quite good and provokes some great  discussions some of them aren't quite up  to snuff and that's what I'm shooting  this new video series about it's sort of  FAQ not only for questions but also for  common issues that arise and the one  that I'm going to talk about here is  when somebody writes a comment on a  video and it's got no discernable  connection whatsoever to the topic of  the video and it's just coming out of  the proverbial left field so there's a  few things to say about that I mean the  first thing is of course it's it's nice  that people get excited enough to to  want to comment but we do want comments  to be more or less relevant to the video  because otherwise there's not a lot of  discussion involved in that and and I  have actually in the past quite not  quite often answered questions that that  are not really on topic but you know  life is short art is long and there's  not a lot of time for for doing that  sort of thing so you know it's important  that that people asking questions they  keep them relevant to the the video and  you know I've done so many videos over a  thousand at this point just in my main  channel that you know if you do a search  and you've got you know some some topic  brewing in the back of your head you  might actually find a video that's more  closely connected with that if there's a  thought that you're dying to to get out  there or a question that you're dying to  ask me the other thing that I would say  is that when it comes to questions that  I'm that I'm being asked that don't seem  to really have much  action to whatever topic is being  discussed in the video there's there's  usually one or two things that that are  going on and and could be better  addressed somewhere else I've already  mentioned that you might find a video  that actually connects with that so you  know if you're asking me about John Paul  Sartre and and freedom on an Aristotle  video and you haven't connected it to  the Aristotle video topic in some way  you can look at my cert videos and maybe  those are the places to actually ask the  question or if you watch the video you  might find your question answered  you know you might also find some more  questions popping up along the way  that's that's okay for philosophy the  other thing is you know I do in fact  have a channel you know there's a stream  just for the channel you can ask me  questions just within the channel and I  think a lot of the questions of these  you know sort of disconnected sorts are  better asked directly to me rather than  cluttering up the comments section of a  video one of the reasons why you would  want to do that other than the fact that  you know you're more likely to get a  response from me is that you know if you  think about what these these videos are  we have the video itself and then  there's all these comments beneath it  and you know if you read through them  sometimes quite a few of them are asking  great questions or making useful points  or making connections to two other  things and you know I want that to be  there as something like a resource for  the other people that are viewing the  videos I want them to be pretty relevant  you know I'm really happy this is a  little bit of a digression I'll come  back to the main topic in a moment I'm  really quite happy when I look at other  YouTube channels and the comment  sections on their videos that my comment  sections are  at the swamps that that really you know  if we use that metaphor that's  circulating around really need some  draining that that quite a few people's  comments sections are you know common  sections are notorious for having lots  of trolls and lots of people saying  terrible things I don't get quite so  much of that but I want to keep it you  know really focused and connected to the  video because other people you know  years from now students lifelong  learners might be using them as a  resource alright so back back to the  main topic you know if you've got some  sort of question or comment and you  really want to get me to to address it  like I said you don't want to go to the  comments section of a video that's  that's just not connected to it you  don't want to just find the latest video  that I've got and then you know plunk it  down there you want to go to the channel  stream and and leave the question or  comment there in in some cases it may be  something that requires a good bit of  discussion and if that's the case then  I'll probably suggest you you know book  me for a tutorial session in other cases  it might not might not take that if I  can answer it fairly quickly with the  resources that I that I have I usually  try to do so so you know this is a you  know a common thing that comes up and I  suppose the last thing that I would say  is if you see this this video appended  as a response to your comment or  question well there's there's a couple  things to keep in mind one is well it's  because you ask something that it may be  relevant but I don't see any possible  immediate connection to what it is that  was being discussed in the video so you  know if you think that I'm wrong about  that maybe you want to follow it up by  making the case or it could be that you  actually did ask something out of left  field because you're really interested  in that sort of thing and you didn't  realize that you were posting it on  that is about some totally different  topic in that case you know don't don't  feel too bad because you will be one of  at this point literally thousands to do  that sort of thing  I have had you know we're now in like  the you know the five digits for a  number of comments on my videos total  and quite a few of them have been in  that sort so you know don't don't take  it too badly and just find the right  place for getting the sorts of answers  that you want the last thing to say is  you know a lot of people ask questions  that I think they can probably answer on  their own by doing a fairly minimal web  search whether in YouTube or you know in  the larger internet and you know I  appreciate that people come to me as as  the the expert on these things but it  you know it's really important for one's  education that one learns what we call  information literacy that is where to  find these quality sources of  information so that you know the other  part of it is is becoming you know  moving yourself beyond the absolute  beginner stage into the intermediate  stage where you know something about the  things that you're asking about you know  you have a great idea about what sort of  questions you want to ask and follow up  on and where to go to get that  information I love seeing that sort of  thing  and so if you know one of these  questions becomes something like a  teachable moment and you're watching  this video you know and you say oh well  that's that's that's me that's what I  actually want for my viewers and  subscribers to get to the point where  they probably don't need to ask me a lot  of questions or you know if they do  they're all relevant because they're  already engaged in the process  themselves of figuring out where to find  the answers to the questions that  they've got  [Music]

